# Work Left with Estimation — 2026-07-27
> Пометка 14.08.26: упоминания ветки `DpoImplementation` в этом файле — historical; ветка-канон = `codex/dpo-implementation` (см. 14.08.26WorkLeft-05-branch-reconciliation-codex-dpo-implementation.md).

**What this report is.** This is the remaining-work estimate for the DPO-implementation project (the self-building "learning dynamic graph" that is meant to construct its own preference-training pairs and train itself on them). It is derived from the status audit in `report status of development 27.7.26.md` (Findings 1–43), which mapped, against the live code at git commit `0f221ac89cff57af4205d342ead51213425c0c45` (branch `DpoImplementation`, working tree clean), what is finished and what is not.

**Scope — CODE ONLY (bucket 1).** By explicit instruction, this report covers only the unfinished *code* — the started-but-incomplete pieces and the concrete work to finish each. It deliberately does NOT cover the training part (enumerating training sources/examples and testing them locally with the open model Qwen3.5-9B); that "training bucket" is deferred until the code fixes are lined up, and will be its own separate report. Wherever finishing a code piece crosses into the not-yet-started training work, the entry marks that boundary and stops.

**What each entry provides (per unfinished-code item):**
1. **The problem** — in plain human language, with the exact `file:line` evidence (every code identifier translated on first use).
2. **The fix** — the concrete code change that finishes the piece (what it must become).
3. **A POSITIVE example and a NEGATIVE example** — in the spirit of preference training (the good "chosen" behaviour/output the fix must produce, and the plausible-but-wrong "rejected" behaviour the fix must prevent).
4. **Tests that should PASS and tests that should FAIL** — the concrete checks that prove the fix, framed as which assertions must go green and which must go red, confirmable without running any training.
5. **Effort estimate** — a plain size for the code work, with the deferred-training boundary called out separately where it applies.

**Method.** One architect (Fable) designs the estimation task-fronts; then executors (Opus) run one at a time, each appending exactly one entry below, verified on disk by the orchestrator. Read-only against the audited repo; the code tree stays clean at `0f221ac89`. Every entry is written in plain human language with all machine shortcodes translated.

