### WL-02 — The LoRA correction layers are built and then thrown away: make the model's answer path actually run through them

**1. The problem.**

LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) is the cheap way to teach a large model: you freeze the model's original weights so they never change, then bolt on small extra "correction" matrices called adapters, and during teaching you train only those adapters. When the model later answers a question, each layer is supposed to compute its normal output PLUS the adapter's learned correction. If that correction is never added while the model computes an answer, everything the adapters learned is silently discarded — the model answers exactly as if LoRA had never happened. In this codebase the adapters are built but never stand in the answer path, so all teaching would be thrown away. Every line below was re-read at commit `0f221ac89cff57af4205d342ead51213425c0c45`.

The adapter factory — the helper that is supposed to prepare a model for LoRA teaching — is `LoraApplicator` in `10.LoRA/LoraApplicator.cs`. Its own header comment (`:11–14`), written in Russian, ends with the sentence "Forward pass базовой модели не меняется; адаптеры применяются снаружи" — literally "the base model's forward pass is not changed; the adapters are applied from the outside" ("forward pass" is the computation that turns input text into the model's next-word scores). That comment is a written admission that the glue is missing and is expected to be added by some outside caller that, as shown below, does not exist.

`LoraApplicator.Apply` (`:17–45`) does three things: it freezes every base weight so none can be trained (`:20–21`, `RequiresGrad = false`); it walks each transformer block's attention sub-layer and, by reflection, only READS out each internal linear projection (helper `GetNamedLinears`, `:48–59`, where `field.GetValue(parent)` at `:57` reads a field but writes nothing back); and for each projection whose name matches the config it builds a new `LoraLinear` adapter and adds it to a list (`:40`). It then returns that list (`:44`, `return adapters;`) — a detached collection of wrappers with no connection to the running model. Nothing is ever written back into the attention layer, and nothing is registered on the model.

The adapter object itself, `LoraLinear` in `10.LoRA/LoraLinear.cs`, holds the only trainable weights in the whole scheme: two matrices named `lora_a` and `lora_b` (registered at `:47–48`), where `lora_b` starts as all zeros so the correction starts at zero. Its `Forward` method (`:52–63`) computes the correct intended math — the base layer's output plus the scaled low-rank correction (`:63`, `TensorOps.Add(baseOut, lora)`). But it has a latent defect even if it were wired in: its dropout call hard-codes `training: true` (`:57`, `_dropout.Forward(x, training: true)`). Dropout is a teaching-time noise trick that randomly zeroes part of the input to prevent over-fitting; it MUST be off when the model answers, or every answer gets random noise injected and becomes non-deterministic and degraded. Because that flag is a hard-coded constant instead of "on while teaching, off while answering", this adapter would corrupt answers if it were ever used at answer time (harmless today only because it is never called).

The answer path never touches an adapter. `LlmModel.Forward` (`03.TransformerModel/LlmModel.cs:47–54`) runs token embedding, then a loop that calls `block.Forward(x)` over each block (`:50–51`), then a final norm and output projection. Each `TransformerBlock.Forward` (`03.TransformerModel/TransformerBlock.cs:40–46`) calls its own private attention field directly (`:43`, `_attn.Forward(...)`). That attention layer, `CausalSelfAttention` (`03.TransformerModel/Layers/CausalSelfAttention.cs`), holds its four projections as `private readonly Linear _qProj/_kProj/_vProj/_oProj` (`:17–20`) — "private readonly" meaning nothing outside the class can replace them — and its `Forward` calls those fields directly (`:53–55` for the query/key/value projections and `:75` for the output projection). Because the fields are private and read-only, and because `LoraApplicator` only reads them, no line anywhere in the answer path ever calls a `LoraLinear`. The `Attention` property (`TransformerBlock.cs:25`, commented "public access for LoraApplicator") exists only so the factory can READ the projections, not replace them.

The preference trainer is affected the same way. `DpoTrainer` (`11.DPO/DpoTrainer.cs`) is the step that teaches the model from "chosen vs rejected" answer pairs (DPO = Direct Preference Optimization). It scores the model in `LogProbs` (`:242`, `model.Forward(ids)`) and computes gradients in `BackwardLogProbs` (`:279`, `model.Forward(ids)`) — both through the same frozen-base `model.Forward`. Since that forward never routes through an adapter, and since `Apply` froze every base weight and never registered the adapters onto the model, the optimizer neither trains nor uses the adapters at any point.

Both production callers build the adapters and drop them. In `18.CapabilityTraining/LlmIngredientLogicalUnits.cs`, line `:344` calls `var adapters = LoraApplicator.Apply(...)` under a comment claiming it makes "the teaching path include the adapter tier," but the list is used for exactly one thing — its element count is copied into a report field (`:393`, `adapters.Count` into the result record). It is never handed to the trainer or the model. In `18.CapabilityTraining/TeachingRegistryPersistence.cs`, line `:322` calls `LoraApplicator.Apply(model, ...)` and discards the return value outright, then reads embeddings through the same base-only forward path.

**2. The fix.**

Make the four attention projections swappable and wire the adapters into the live model. Concretely:

- In `CausalSelfAttention` (`03.TransformerModel/Layers/CausalSelfAttention.cs:17–20`), stop declaring the four projections as `private readonly`. Give them a settable slot, or add a small method like `ReplaceProjection(name, module)` that overwrites `_qProj` / `_kProj` / `_vProj` / `_oProj` with a replacement. Because `LoraLinear.Forward` returns the same shape as `Linear.Forward`, the existing `Forward` (`:53–55`, `:75`) then automatically computes base + correction with no other change.
- In `LoraApplicator.Apply` (`10.LoRA/LoraApplicator.cs`), after building each `LoraLinear` (`:40`), write it back into the matching attention slot AND register the adapter as a sub-module of the model, so its two trainable matrices `lora_a` and `lora_b` appear in `model.Parameters()`. Today `Apply` only reads the projection field (`:57`) and returns a detached list (`:44`); it must instead install the adapter and attach its parameters to the model's parameter tree.
- In `LoraLinear.Forward` (`10.LoRA/LoraLinear.cs:57`), replace the hard-coded `training: true` with a real teach/answer flag threaded through the forward path, so dropout is on while teaching and off while answering.
- Stop dropping the adapters in both callers. `LlmIngredientLogicalUnits.cs:344` must let the adapter parameters actually reach the trainer/optimizer instead of only copying `adapters.Count` at `:393`; `TeachingRegistryPersistence.cs:322` must keep and use the installed adapters instead of discarding the return value.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).**

GOOD — the "chosen" behaviour the fix must produce: build a model, call `LoraApplicator.Apply` to install the adapters, then manually set one adapter's second matrix (`lora_b`) to a non-zero value (bypassing any training). Run the same input through the model twice. The model's next-word scores now DIFFER from the pre-adapter model's scores — proof the correction is actually being added in the answer path — and two repeated answer-mode calls produce identical scores — proof dropout is off when answering.

BAD — the "rejected" behaviour the fix must forbid (this is exactly what happens today): call `Apply`, set `lora_b` non-zero, and the model's output is bit-for-bit identical to a model where LoRA never happened, because the answer path never runs through the adapter. Equally rejected: a training report that proudly announces "adapter count: 4" while the training step updates zero adapter weights — which is precisely the `adapters.Count` report field at `LlmIngredientLogicalUnits.cs:393` over a training loop that touches only frozen base weights.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL.**

All of these are single-forward-pass checks — no training run is involved.

PASS after the fix:
- Build a model, `Apply`, set an adapter's `lora_b` to non-zero, and assert `model.Forward(sameInput)` yields DIFFERENT next-word scores than the same model before the adapter existed (or with `lora_b` = 0). Today the scores are identical, proving the adapter is ignored.
- Assert `model.Parameters()` contains the adapter matrices (`lora_a`, `lora_b`) after `Apply`. Today they are absent because the adapters are detached from the model.
- Assert answer-mode `Forward` is deterministic: a fixed input gives identical output across repeated calls (dropout off). Today this cannot even be expressed because dropout is hard-wired on.

These new assertions belong in the existing LoRA suite `Tests/LoraTests.cs` (which today covers only the adapter in isolation — e.g. `LoraLinear_NonZeroLoraB_ChangesOutput` tests `LoraLinear.Forward` by itself, not through the model), reinforced by `Tests/TransformerModelTests.cs` (which owns the `LlmModel_Forward…` model-level checks).

FAIL after the fix — any test pinning today's broken behaviour, which must be re-pointed at the honest behaviour:
- Any test asserting identical model logits after installing a non-zero adapter.
- `Tests/LoraTests.cs` → `LoraApplicator_Apply_FreezesAllBaseParams` currently asserts that EVERY parameter in `model.Parameters()` is frozen after `Apply`. Once the adapter matrices are registered on the model, `lora_a`/`lora_b` must be trainable, so this test's "all parameters frozen" claim becomes false and must be narrowed to "all BASE parameters frozen, adapter parameters trainable."
- Any test in `Tests/DpoTrainerTests.cs` or `Tests/CapabilityTrainingSeparationTests.cs` / `Tests/TeachingRegistryPersistenceTests.cs` that pins the current "adapters counted but never in the parameter list / never in the forward path" behaviour must be re-pointed.

**5. Effort estimate.**

MEDIUM, ~6–10 hours, CODE ONLY. Rationale: the wiring itself is small (swap four projection slots, register two matrices, thread one boolean flag), but it touches the attention layer's field declarations, the adapter factory, both callers, and several existing pinning tests that assert today's broken behaviour and must be corrected in the same change.

**BOUNDARY — TRAINING WORK, DEFERRED.** Proving that the now-wired adapters actually improve the model's answers — an actual DPO/LoRA teaching run — is deferred training work. Nothing beyond this boundary is estimated in this entry. STOP.

