### WL-03 — The incremental trainer reports "trained" without training anything: build its four-step body and forbid success without a real file

**1. The problem.**

The "incremental trainer" (`14.ContinuousLearning/Training/IncrementalTrainer.cs`) is the service meant to run one small extra-training pass over newly accumulated examples for a single domain (for example "CODE_Q", the code-question domain) and save the result as a LoRA adapter file — LoRA being the technique that freezes the big base model and trains only a tiny pair of extra matrices. Its one method, `TrainAsync` (the method that is supposed to do the training run; lines 43-58), is a hollow pipe. It checks its inputs — an empty domain name returns a failure carrying the code `EMPTY_DOMAIN` (lines 48-49; the return type `OperationResult<string>` is this repo's success-or-failure wrapper that carries either a value or an error code), and zero examples returns `EMPTY_EXAMPLES` (lines 50-51). Then it composes a path string like `adapters/CODE_Q.lora` (line 55), awaits an already-finished empty operation that does nothing (`await Task.CompletedTask`, line 56), and returns success with the path to a file that was never written (line 57). No model is loaded, no weights change, no adapter is produced, nothing is saved.

The header comment (lines 11-21) promises a four-step contract — (1) apply LoRA adapters to a live model, (2) one next-word-prediction pass per training example, (3) one preference step per preferred-answer/rejected-answer pair, (4) save the `.lora` file — and then defers all of it with a comment (lines 19-20) that points at `16.AutonomousLearning.DataPipeline` "where a live TransformerModel.LlmModel is constructed" (LlmModel being the actual from-scratch neural network). That pointer is FALSE, confirmed two ways at this HEAD: there is no `DataPipeline` folder anywhere (a directory search returns nothing), and no file under `16.AutonomousLearning` constructs the model (a grep for `new LlmModel` under that folder returns zero non-test hits).

The fake success travels far and is recorded as real. The replay-buffer wrapper (`16.AutonomousLearning/Training/AutonomousTrainer.cs`; a replay buffer re-mixes old examples with new ones to avoid forgetting) calls the hollow trainer (line 66) and, because it reports success, advances its buffer "ONLY on successful training" (lines 72-75) on the strength of a no-op. The nightly cycle (`16.AutonomousLearning/Pipeline/AutonomousLearningPipeline.cs`) takes the fake path (line 125), wraps it in a `ModelAdapter` record (the record standing for "one trained adapter file") stamped with a real wall-clock "trained at" timestamp (lines 131-135), passes a promotion gate (lines 142-145), and registers it (line 147). The registry never checks the file exists — `DomainModelRegistry.Register` (`14.ContinuousLearning/Registry/DomainModelRegistry.cs:33-39`) only null-checks the record, then stores it. The one gate that could object is the accuracy check, but its interface `IAccuracyEvaluator` (the accuracy-measuring contract) has NO production implementation at all — the only implementer is a scripted test double, `SequentialAccuracyAL` (`Tests/AutonomousLearningTests.cs:590`), that just hands back pre-set numbers in sequence. Net: a no-op becomes a registered, timestamped "trained" adapter pointing at nothing.

The building blocks for a real body already exist elsewhere: the adapter factory `LoraApplicator.Apply` (`10.LoRA/LoraApplicator.cs:17`) freezes the base model and returns the trainable adapter layers; the preference step `DpoTrainer.Step` (`11.DPO/DpoTrainer.cs:38`) runs one preferred-vs-rejected update; the whole-model saver `LlmModel.SaveCheckpoint` (`03.TransformerModel/LlmModel.cs:66`) writes the full network to disk. The one missing block is Step 4's adapter-weight serializer: the two learned matrices inside an adapter layer are private with no getter (`private readonly Parameter _loraA` / `_loraB`, `10.LoRA/LoraLinear.cs:18-19`), and a repo-wide search finds no code anywhere that writes an actual `.lora` weight file.

**2. The fix.**

Give `TrainAsync` a real body that fulfils its own four-step contract, all as glue over existing primitives:

- Construct or inject the neural network (`LlmModel`): the trainer holds no model and never builds one today. Add it as a constructor dependency, or build it from a config using the same `new LlmModel(config)` used on other production paths.
- Call the adapter factory: `LoraApplicator.Apply(model, DefaultConfig)` — the config is already present on the class (rank 8 / alpha 16, lines 25-31) — to freeze the base and get back the trainable adapter layers.
- Write the per-example next-word-prediction training loop over the adapter parameters only: for each training example, tokenize its prompt and response, run the model forward, compute the standard next-word (cross-entropy) loss, run backward, and step the optimizer over the adapter layers' parameters — the same forward → backward → optimizer-step machinery the sibling preference trainer already uses.
- Convert the currently-ignored preference pairs into a batch and call the preference trainer's step: today the `dpoPairs` argument (a list of preferred-answer/rejected-answer examples) is silently dropped. Instead tokenize each pair into the tokenized form the preference trainer consumes (`DpoBatch`) and call `DpoTrainer.Step` (or its many-batch variant `StepMany`).
- ADD the missing adapter-weight serializer: give the adapter layer (`LoraLinear`) public read access to its two matrices, or a save method on it, that writes the `{domain}.lora` file. No such capability exists today.
- Add a post-condition so the method can NEVER return success unless the file exists on disk: after writing, verify the file is present and non-empty at the returned path and return failure otherwise — write-then-verify.
- Rewrite the false header comment (lines 19-20) so it no longer points at a non-existent `DataPipeline` that builds no model.

Honesty ripple: once success requires a real file on disk, every downstream consumer becomes honest with no further change. The replay-buffer wrapper only advances on a real adapter; the nightly cycle only stamps and registers a `ModelAdapter` that corresponds to a real file; and the registry — which trusts the trainer's success — can no longer hold a "trained at" record pointing at nothing.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).**

GOOD — the "chosen" behaviour the fix must produce: calling the trainer for domain "CODE_Q" with one example produces a non-empty `adapters/CODE_Q.lora` file on disk, and ONLY THEN reports success with that path.

BAD — the "rejected" behaviour the fix must make impossible (this is exactly what happens today): the trainer returns success plus a path, no file exists at that path, and ten minutes later the registry holds an officially "trained at 2026-07-28T…" adapter record pointing at nothing.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL.**

All of these are single-step checks — no training run is involved; one forward pass, one preference step, and one save are enough.

PASS after the fix:
- A one-example call leaves a non-empty `.lora` file at the returned path (assert the file exists and its length is greater than zero). This new test belongs in `Tests/ContinuousLearningTests.cs`, alongside the existing incremental-trainer block — its `IncrementalTrainer_EmptyExamples_ReturnsFailure` (line 220) and `IncrementalTrainer_UsesLoraApplicator_NotCustomGradients` (line 245) stay green.
- A call whose write is blocked (for example a read-only or non-writable adapter directory) returns failure, not success.
- The preference-pairs argument is consumed — it reaches the preference trainer rather than being silently dropped (assert via a spy/fake preference trainer, or that a preference checkpoint or metric was produced).

FAIL after the fix — any test that pins today's behaviour:
- Success with no file on disk.
- Success with the `dpoPairs` argument ignored.

Honest note: no such pinning test exists on the real class today. Every success-path test substitutes a stub, `StubIncrementalTrainerAL` (`Tests/AutonomousLearningTests.cs:663`), so the hollow success is currently untested; the only real-class test today is the empty-examples failure guard (`Tests/ContinuousLearningTests.cs:220`), which survives. The FAIL set is therefore the guard tests one adds to lock the fix in, plus retiring the scripted accuracy double (`SequentialAccuracyAL`, `Tests/AutonomousLearningTests.cs:590`) in favour of a gate that actually reads the adapter file.

**5. Effort estimate.**

MEDIUM, ~10–16 hours, CODE ONLY. Rationale: model construction, the adapter-factory call, and the preference-step wiring are thin glue over existing primitives, but the per-example next-word-prediction loss loop and the brand-new adapter-weight serializer (the adapter has no serialization at all today) are net-new code and dominate the estimate.

**BOUNDARY — TRAINING WORK, DEFERRED.** Actually fine-tuning on real accumulated examples — driving the loss down over real steps to produce a genuinely better adapter — is deferred training work. Nothing beyond this boundary is estimated in this entry. STOP.

