### WL-04 — A finished from-scratch training loop that nothing ever starts: write the ignition key and connect two dead knobs

**1. The problem.**

Every file and line below was re-read at the audited commit 0f221ac89 (branch DpoImplementation) and confirmed exact there. Identifiers are translated to plain English at first use.

"Pre-training" is the first and largest teaching stage of a language model: you feed it a mountain of raw text, cut it into batches, ask the still-ignorant model to predict the next word, measure how wrong it was (the "loss"), work out which direction each internal weight should move to be less wrong (the "gradients"), nudge the weights with an optimizer (the algorithm that turns gradients into weight changes), and every so often save a "checkpoint" (a snapshot of the weights to disk). The folder `04.TrainingLoop` holds a complete, hand-written version of exactly this — a class named "Trainer" (`04.TrainingLoop\Trainer.cs:24`). It is finished, correct code that nothing ever starts.

The proof of deadness: a whole-repo search (excluding the build outputs `bin`/`obj`, the stale worktree copies, and treating the `Tests` folder as test-only) for anyone writing `new Trainer(` matches zero lines. No production file and no test constructs it. The class exists; nobody ignites it.

What the dead loop already does correctly — this is real gradient code, not a stub:
- The constructor `Trainer(TrainingConfig cfg)` (Trainer.cs:30) builds the learning-rate schedule — the plan for how fast the model learns over time, a warm-up ramp then a cosine fade — inside itself (line 33).
- The loop `TrainAsync(model, optimizer, loader, ...)` (Trainer.cs:36) creates the checkpoint folder (line 39), then runs while the step counter is below the configured total (line 41). Each step pulls a batch (line 49), runs the forward pass to get the model's raw next-word scores or "logits" (line 56), computes the cross-entropy loss (line 57), and back-propagates with hand-written gradient math (line 61).
- That gradient math (`Trainer.cs:97-130`) is textbook-correct, not a placeholder: it takes the softmax of each row and sets the gradient to "softmax minus one-hot" (lines 121-126) — the exact derivative of cross-entropy with respect to the logits — then pushes it through the network (line 129).
- It then clips the gradient to stop it exploding (line 65), reads the current learning rate from the schedule (line 67), takes one optimizer step (line 68), and zeroes the gradients (line 69).
- It saves the checkpoint (lines 75-76) BEFORE it advances the step counter `_globalStep++` (line 78) — the repo's "write the record before you move on" rule.

So the loop is whole and coherent. The single thing wrong with it is that no one calls it.

Every ingredient a starter needs already exists and compiles:
- The model — a from-scratch decoder-only transformer named "LlmModel" (`03.TransformerModel\LlmModel.cs:28`), constructed on real production paths elsewhere in the repo (for example `18.CapabilityTraining\DefaultLogicalCapabilityTrainer.cs`, `42.ProjectWikiDomain\ProjectWikiDpoMetricRunner.cs`, and an experiments runner's `Program.cs`).
- The batch loader — "DataLoader" (`02.DatasetPipeline\DataLoader.cs:22`), which streams training batches from disk. Two honest caveats: nothing ever constructs it either (a repo-wide `new DataLoader(` matches zero lines), and it needs a folder that already holds tokenized data files on disk — it throws immediately if the folder has none (lines 27-28).
- The optimizer — "AdamW", Adam with decoupled weight decay (`04.TrainingLoop\Optimizers\AdamW.cs:30`). Unlike the pre-training path, this one IS built in production — but only by the preference trainer at `11.DPO\DpoTrainer.cs:32`.
- The settings record — "TrainingConfig", every hyperparameter in one place with every field defaulted (`04.TrainingLoop\TrainingConfig.cs:11-24`). A repo-wide `new TrainingConfig(` matches zero lines, so it is never instantiated; but because every field has a default it is ready to use as-is.
- The learning-rate schedule — already built inside the Trainer constructor itself (line 33).

Two "dead knobs" to state precisely and honestly:
- Dead knob 1 — the config's weight-decay value. "Weight decay" is a small pull that shrinks weights every step to discourage over-fitting. The config sets it to 0.1 (`TrainingConfig.cs:21`). That value is never handed to any optimizer: a repo-wide search for the config's `WeightDecay` property being read finds only its own definition (line 21) and an unrelated test method's name (`AdamWTests.cs:52`) — no code passes it anywhere. This is a WIRING gap, not broken arithmetic: the decay MATH inside the optimizer is live and unit-tested — `AdamW.cs:73` applies `w *= 1 - lr * weightDecay` every step, and `Tests\AdamWTests.cs:52-60` starts a weight at 10 with a zero gradient and proves it shrinks below 10.
- Dead knob 2 — the optimizer's learning-rate constructor argument. AdamW's constructor takes a learning-rate argument (`AdamW.cs:30`) that is never stored in any field (the only fields are the two momentum coefficients, the weight decay, and the epsilon). It is harmless today because every step is handed an explicit rate from the schedule — but a caller who writes `new AdamW(lr: 3e-4)` expecting the optimizer to remember that rate would be silently wrong.

The proof the wiring pattern works is the living twin one level over: the preference trainer "DpoTrainer" (`11.DPO\DpoTrainer.cs:28-36`) builds an LlmModel, an AdamW (line 32), and a checkpoint directory, then drives the same forward → hand-written backward → optimizer-step → zero-gradients loop shape (lines 38-62) — on a real production path (it is constructed in `18.CapabilityTraining`, `42.ProjectWikiDomain`, and an experiments runner's `Program.cs`). The pre-training starter is structurally simpler than this twin; only it is absent.

**2. The fix.**

Add ONE new production (non-test) entry point — a single glue file — that news up the ingredients and starts the loop. It must:
1. build the settings record: `var cfg = new TrainingConfig { CheckpointDir = ..., TotalSteps = ..., BatchSize = ..., SeqLen = ..., ... };`
2. build the model: `var model = new LlmModel(modelConfig);`
3. build the optimizer PASSING the config's weight decay — `var optimizer = new AdamW(weightDecay: cfg.WeightDecay, ...);` — which simultaneously closes dead knob 1, because now the configured 0.1 actually reaches the optimizer instead of sitting inert;
4. build the batch loader over a folder of tokenized data files: `var loader = new DataLoader(dataDir, cfg.BatchSize, cfg.SeqLen);`
5. start the loop: `await new Trainer(cfg).TrainAsync(model, optimizer, loader);`

And separately close dead knob 2: either store the optimizer constructor's learning-rate argument in a field and use it as the default rate when a step is not handed an explicit one, or remove the argument entirely — so no caller can pass `lr:` and be silently ignored. The living twin at `DpoTrainer.cs:28-36` proves this exact construct-and-drive pattern already works on a production path.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).**

GOOD — the "chosen" behaviour the fix must produce: one smoke iteration of the loop over a tiny synthetic batch (a hand-built handful of token ids, no real dataset) changes at least one model weight and writes a checkpoint file into the configured checkpoint folder; and because the config's weight decay is now wired through, the optimizer provably decays weights by the configured 0.1 — a weight of 10 with a zero gradient shrinks (exactly what the existing decay test at `AdamWTests.cs:52-60` already demonstrates for the optimizer in isolation).

BAD — the "rejected" behaviour the fix must make impossible: a developer "configures" weight decay 0.1 in the settings record and the optimizer silently uses a hard-coded value instead (today the one production optimizer, at `DpoTrainer.cs:32`, hard-codes `weightDecay: 0.01f` and ignores any config); or a developer writes `new AdamW(lr: 3e-4)` and the learning rate is silently discarded because the constructor never stores it.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL.** No real training run — one forward, one backward, one optimizer step, and one save are enough.

PASS after the fix:
- A repo-wide search for `new Trainer(` finds a caller on a production (non-test) path. Today it finds nothing — that is the proof of deadness.
- A one-iteration smoke test — a tiny model and a tiny synthetic batch — asserts that at least one model parameter changed after the step AND that a checkpoint file appeared in the configured folder.
- A test (or a source scan for the config's `WeightDecay` being read outside `TrainingConfig.cs`) shows the configured weight-decay value demonstrably reaching the optimizer's constructor.
- The existing decay-math test `Tests\AdamWTests.cs:52` stays green (the fix does not touch the optimizer's arithmetic).

FAIL after the fix — any assertion that pins today's deadness, and which must be retired when the fix lands:
- Any assertion that zero `Trainer` constructions exist, or that zero `TrainingConfig` instantiations exist.
- Any assertion that the config's weight-decay value is read nowhere outside its own definition.

**5. Effort estimate.** SMALL, roughly 3-6 hours, CODE ONLY. Rationale: this is the smallest of the wiring entries — five constructor calls plus one await in a single new glue file, one two-line change to remember-or-remove the optimizer's learning-rate argument, and one smoke test, all over parts that already exist and compile.
**BOUNDARY — TRAINING WORK, DEFERRED.** Producing and placing the tokenized training data files on disk, and actually running the pre-training to drive the loss down and emit real checkpoints, is deferred training work; nothing beyond this boundary is estimated here. STOP.

