### WL-05 — The self-measuring graph engine is never built by any running process: add the production socket and switch on the two measurement doors that need no training

**1. The problem.**

XIIGen ships a "dynamic graph machine" — a self-improving graph engine that is supposed to (a) route a piece of work through a graph of nodes, (b) fan one task out into several parallel subtasks and merge their results back, and (c) force every quality number the system produces through eleven mandatory "measurement doors" (a measurement door is a contract gate that a number must pass before it is allowed to count). All of that machinery is finished, compiles, and is covered by tests. None of it is ever built or driven by a running server. It is a museum exhibit: complete, polished, behind glass, connected to nothing.

Three things are true at this exact commit, each re-read line by line:

- The single place in the whole codebase that assembles the machine is one factory method — the "assemble an honestly-untrained machine" method (`CreateHonestUntrained`, `15.OrchestratorModel/Logical/DynamicGraphMachineCompositionFactory.cs:39`). It composes the runtime once and, only if every node is provably untrained (no learned checkpoint, no "final authority" claim, no runtime quality boast), wraps it in the top-level machine object (`new DynamicGraphMachine(...)`, :128–129). A whole-repo search (excluding build output and worktree copies, and treating the `Tests/` folder as tests) shows this method's only callers are the factory itself and one test file, and the machine object is constructed at exactly that one line and nowhere else. So even the machine's honest "I am not switched on yet" refusals can never leave a running server, because nothing running ever builds the object that would speak them.

- The production-facing doorway into the fan-out chain is a pair of public methods on the orchestrator assistant (the object that would drive a request through the graph): the "run the factory-selected target" method (`RunFactorySelectedTargetExecutionAsync`, `15.OrchestratorModel/Logical/LogicalOrchestratorAssistant.cs:471`) and its already-admitted sibling (`RunFactoryAdmittedSelectedTargetExecutionAsync`, :505). A whole-repo search shows the only references to either are the assistant that defines them, one in-slice adapter, and tests — no live request path. The assistant's one genuinely-live method (the ordinary "run a user request" method `RunAsync`, :530) deliberately routes through the legacy/adaptive orchestrator and never calls the fan-out pair. And in the default way the assistant is built, the two graph injection points are left empty: the topology-source argument defaults to null (:126) and the default of the two adaptive-orchestrator constructions passes no node-output source (:422–442), so the dynamic-graph branch stays inert even if the assistant were alive.

- All eleven measurement doors sit behind one coordinator object (`DynamicGraphMeasurementCoordinator`, `21.AgentInfrastructure/Logical/DynamicGraphMeasurementCoordinator.cs`; its own summary at line 11 says it "composes target admission with numeric measurement"). A whole-repo search for any call into that coordinator matches only tests and one standalone developer tool. No server, no routing unit, no business domain reaches in. Meanwhile the only live server in the product — the design-conversion model server (`25.ModelServer/DesignConversion/DesignConversionModelServerApi.cs`), which runs each job through a design-conversion improvement loop (`coordinator.RunAsync`, `44.DesignConversionDomain/DesignConversionThinClientJobRuntime.cs:90`, where that coordinator is a design-conversion object, not the graph) — never names the assistant at all: the assistant type appears zero times anywhere under the model-server or design-conversion-domain folders.

The three seams where the wiring is missing, in order:
- Seam 1 — construction: no live host ever CONSTRUCTS the machine (or the assistant) with real graph sources. The one assembler (`DynamicGraphMachineCompositionFactory.cs:39`) has no production caller.
- Seam 2 — per-request drive: no per-request call ever DRIVES the fan-out run method (`LogicalOrchestratorAssistant.cs:471`). The live "run a user request" method (:530) routes around it.
- Seam 3 — ownership: no host OWNS seams 1 and 2. The only live server (`DesignConversionModelServerApi.cs`) bypasses the whole slice.

Of the eleven doors, exactly two can be exercised with pure code today — untrained-honesty (door 1, `BindL2UntrainedHonesty`, `DynamicGraphMeasurementCoordinator.cs:78`), which measures the ABSENCE of training by confirming the runtime honestly reported "not ready", and run-speed (door 5, `MeasureRunSpeed`, :301), which is pure wall-clock timing of a finished run. The other nine each need an artifact that only a training run creates (a trained subject, a held-out data split, or a learned edge authority) and stay behind the deferred-training line.

**2. The fix.**

Add ONE production caller that owns all three seams, placed either inside the existing design-conversion model server's request path or in a new small orchestration endpoint. It must:
1. once at startup (seam 1): build the honestly-untrained machine by calling the assemble method (`DynamicGraphMachineCompositionFactory.CreateHonestUntrained(storage, profile, adapter)`, `DynamicGraphMachineCompositionFactory.cs:39`) with a real bootstrap profile (real node definitions and edge bindings), OR build the assistant directly (`LogicalOrchestratorAssistant.CreateDefaultCore`, `LogicalOrchestratorAssistant.cs:137/149`) while passing a real topology source and node-output source into the two injection points that are null today (:126, :422–442);
2. per request (seam 2): drive the fan-out run method (`LogicalOrchestratorAssistant.RunFactorySelectedTargetExecutionAsync`, :471) so one task actually fans out into subtasks and merges back;
3. own both (seam 3): host the above inside the live request path so real traffic flows through it.

Then wire the two pure-code doors so they fire on that real traffic:
- untrained-honesty (door 1, `BindL2UntrainedHonesty`, `DynamicGraphMeasurementCoordinator.cs:78`): call it at the moment an untrained baseline node runs, recording "honestly untrained — no fake quality claim";
- run-speed (door 5, `MeasureRunSpeed`, :301): call it on run completion, recording the real elapsed milliseconds.

No training artifacts are required for any of the above.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).**

GOOD — the "chosen" behaviour the fix must produce: a real request reaches the live server; the server constructs the honestly-untrained graph machine and drives the fan-out run to a result; AND the untrained-honesty door records "honestly untrained, no fake quality claim" while the run-speed door records the real elapsed milliseconds of that run. The graph slice is now plugged in and measuring real traffic.

BAD — the "rejected" behaviour the fix must make impossible (this is today's state): the entire graph engine, with all eleven quality doors, exists only as a museum piece; a production request completes with zero graph involvement and zero measurements taken, while the test suite gives the false impression that the machinery is exercised.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL.** No training run is involved.

PASS after the fix:
- A whole-repo search finds a non-test caller of the assemble method (`CreateHonestUntrained`) — today only the factory and one test file match.
- A whole-repo search finds a non-test caller of the fan-out run method (`RunFactorySelectedTargetExecutionAsync`) — today only the assistant, one in-slice adapter, and tests match.
- An integration test driven through the live server's endpoint proves the two pure-code doors (`BindL2UntrainedHonesty` and `MeasureRunSpeed`) are reached from a real request path.
- The assistant type (`LogicalOrchestratorAssistant`) appears at least once under the live server's folder (`25.ModelServer`) — today it appears zero times there.

FAIL after the fix — any assertion that pins today's isolation and must be retired when the fix lands:
- Any assertion that the machine is constructed nowhere outside the factory and tests.
- Any assertion that the assistant type is referenced zero times under the live server folder.

**5. Effort estimate.** MEDIUM, roughly 6–12 hours, CODE ONLY. Rationale: the diff is modest (one construction site, one per-request call, two door calls), but adding a brand-new call seam into a live server's request path is design-sensitive — it touches how requests are routed and how the machine's lifetime is owned, which needs care beyond the line count.
**BOUNDARY — TRAINING WORK, DEFERRED.** The nine remaining doors — ablation, held-out accuracy, novelty, training-speed, edge-authority-control, composite edge-quality, edge-authority-selection-quality, edge-identity, and edge-order — each need a trained subject, a held-out split, or a learned edge authority, none of which exist until a training run is performed. They remain blocked; nothing is estimated for them here beyond noting the block. STOP.

