### WL-06 — The quality gate that can never receive its numbers, the promotion handoff that can never finish, and the thrice-copied 0.03 preference margin

**1. The problem.**

The design-conversion domain — the subsystem in `44.DesignConversionDomain` that turns a design mock-up (a Figma or Canva frame) into HTML/CSS code — is the most training-disciplined corner of this repository, yet three of its finishing steps ("tails") are left unwired. Every identifier below is translated on first use.

*Tail 1 — the measurement gate can never receive its numbers.* A "measurement gate" is the checkpoint that is supposed to demand, before a trained result counts, that "the trained thing scored well on held-out examples (test examples deliberately kept away from training) and stayed inside its runtime, cost, and memory budgets." The gate's evaluator only runs when the training request carries a numbers-object called a `MeasurementGateRequest` (the request that carries the thresholds — the required score, the maximum runtime in milliseconds, the maximum cost in US dollars, the maximum peak RAM in megabytes — plus the real held-out metrics). But that field is OPTIONAL and defaults to null on the production training options: `DesignConversionMeasurementGateRequest? MeasurementGateRequest = null` (`DesignConversionTrainingPipeline.cs:21`). The training gate runs the evaluator only when the field is non-null: `options.MeasurementGateRequest is null ? null : _measurementGate.Evaluate(...)` (`DesignConversionTrainingMeasurementGate.cs:391–393`). And every construction of that request, or of its thresholds record `DesignConversionMeasurementGateThresholds` (`DesignConversionMeasurementGate.cs:32–36`), anywhere in the tree lives in Tests — a repo-wide grep of `new DesignConversionMeasurementGateRequest` / `new DesignConversionMeasurementGateThresholds` matches only test files (nine of them); the single non-test match is the `= null` default itself. So in the running product the gate is permanently FAIL-CLOSED (blocks whenever in doubt), never satisfied — because nobody ever hands it numbers.

Be precise about the honesty here: the four `false` flags at `DesignConversionMeasurementGate.cs:214–218` are NOT an "always pass" switch. They are the honest "no thresholds were supplied, so fail for missing thresholds" branch — reached only inside `if (thresholds is null)`, which first records the failure reason `MEASUREMENT_GATE_THRESHOLDS_REQUIRED` (:211–213). When real numbers ARE supplied, the same method (`ValidateThresholds`) turns each flag on by numeric validity — a valid score in 0..1, a positive runtime budget, a non-negative cost budget, a positive RAM budget (:221–238). Because the gate never passes on the product path, `readyForTraining` stays false (it requires the gate to pass, `DesignConversionTrainingMeasurementGate.cs:415–421`), and downstream promotion is blocked.

*Tail 2 — the promotion handoff can never finish.* The "promotion handoff" is the step that ships a promoted, trained result (a "checkpoint" — a saved file of the model's state) to the model server that will serve it. This handoff IS called in production: `JsonlDesignConversionLocalArtifactDb` constructs and invokes the publisher `new DesignConversionPromotionPublisher(publicationUploader).PublishAsync(...)` at `DesignConversionLocalArtifactDb.cs:3028`, behind a real model-server HTTP endpoint. But it can never COMPLETE, for two reasons. First, the default uploader is a local no-op: `publicationUploader = _promotionUploader ?? new LocalOnlyTrainingIntakePromotionUploader()` (`DesignConversionLocalArtifactDb.cs:3027`), and that local uploader writes a receipt and explicitly attempts NO upload — it returns `UploadAttempted = false` with the evidence tag `EXTERNAL_CLOUD_UPLOAD_NOT_ATTEMPTED` (:7752–7776). The real network uploader (`DesignConversionModelServerPromotionHttpUploader`) is wired only when the host is configured with a `PromotionHttpUploaderOptions` record (`DesignConversionModelServerAspNetHostStartup.cs:1250–1259`), and every construction of that options record lives in Tests. Second, even with a real uploader, the unpassable Tail-1 gate rejects first: when the measurement gate did not pass, `PublishAsync` replaces the decision with Reject and never reaches the uploader (`DesignConversionPromotion.cs:245–260`).

*Tail 3 — the thrice-copied 0.03 preference margin.* A "preference margin" is the number saying how much more the model must prefer the good ("chosen") answer over the bad ("rejected") one. Three hard-coded `0.03` literals sit in the translation machine (`27.TranslationDomain/TranslationMachine.cs`): `RewardMargin: 0.03` (:75), a ternary `? 0.03` (:130), and `_trainingHistory.Count > 0 ? 0.03 : 0.0` (:192). Meanwhile the central preference trainer — the DPO (Direct Preference Optimization) trainer at `11.DPO/DpoTrainer.cs` — owns NO minimum-margin constant at all: its two public constants at :19–20 are merely checkpoint file-name helpers (`PolicyCheckpointFilePrefix`, `PolicyCheckpointFileExtension`), its `Beta` at :18 is a private KL-penalty coefficient (a different hyper-parameter), and its real margins are COMPUTED at run time (`float margin = chosenReward - rejectedReward`, :51/:70, averaged as `marginSum / count` at :127/:173). So the same magic 0.03 is copy-pasted in three places with no shared source of truth.

**2. The fix.** Three concrete, code-only changes (the threshold NUMBERS themselves are out of scope — see section 5).

(i) *Make the measurement-gate request a REQUIRED, configuration-sourced input on the production training/promotion path.* Change the production path so that `MeasurementGateRequest` is populated from a configuration document — the thresholds (required score, max runtime ms, max cost USD, max peak RAM MB) read from a config record — rather than left null at `DesignConversionTrainingPipeline.cs:21`. With the request supplied, `DesignConversionTrainingMeasurementGate.cs:391–393` stops resolving `measurementResult` to null on the product path and the evaluator actually runs; `ValidateThresholds` then takes its populated branch (`DesignConversionMeasurementGate.cs:221–238`) and turns the four validity flags on by real numeric validity. The four `false` fallbacks at :214–218 stay exactly as they are — they remain the honest "no thresholds" branch. (What number goes in the config document is deferred — section 5.)

(ii) *Wire a real uploader from host configuration on the production promotion path.* Supply the `PromotionHttpUploaderOptions` record OUTSIDE Tests so the host constructs the real network uploader `DesignConversionModelServerPromotionHttpUploader` (`DesignConversionModelServerAspNetHostStartup.cs:1250–1259`) and hands it to the artifact DB as `_promotionUploader`, instead of falling through to the `LocalOnlyTrainingIntakePromotionUploader` no-op at `DesignConversionLocalArtifactDb.cs:3027` / `:7752–7776`. Once Tail-1's gate can pass, `PublishAsync` no longer short-circuits to Reject at `DesignConversionPromotion.cs:245–260` and reaches the real uploader.

(iii) *Add ONE canonical constant and point all three literals at it.* Add `public const double MinimumPreferenceMargin = 0.03;` to `DpoTrainer` (`11.DPO/DpoTrainer.cs`), alongside its existing public constants at :19–20. Type it `double` (not `float` like `Beta`) so it feeds the double-typed `RewardMargin` fields directly with no precision-losing cast (`00.Core/LearningMachineContracts.cs:58` and `:103`). Then replace the literals at `TranslationMachine.cs:75`, `:130`, `:192` with `DpoTrainer.MinimumPreferenceMargin`. No new project reference is needed — `27.TranslationDomain/27.TranslationDomain.csproj:7` already references `..\11.DPO\DPO.csproj` — so this is one `using DPO;` directive plus three one-token replacements.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).**

GOOD — the "chosen" answer the fix should make normal: a production training run receives a thresholds object from configuration; the measurement gate evaluates REAL held-out numbers and its verdict — pass OR fail — is a genuine verdict earned against real data; when it passes, the publisher reaches the real network uploader and the promoted checkpoint actually ships to the model server; and the translation machine's reported preference margin traces back to one named constant (`DpoTrainer.MinimumPreferenceMargin`).

BAD — the "rejected" answer the fix must make impossible: a measurement gate that "fails" forever solely because nobody ever handed it numbers — a permanently-blocked pipeline that is indistinguishable, from the outside, from a strict one that fails on merit; a "promotion" whose upload step is a receipt-writing no-op that never leaves the machine; and the same magic 0.03 copy-pasted in three sites, free to drift apart independently.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL** (no training runs required for any of these).

PASS after the fix:
- A repo-wide grep (excluding bin, obj, worktrees, Tests) finds a NON-test construction of `DesignConversionMeasurementGateRequest` and/or `DesignConversionMeasurementGateThresholds`.
- On the product path, the gate call at `DesignConversionTrainingMeasurementGate.cs:391–393` no longer resolves `measurementResult` to null.
- A non-test construction of `DesignConversionModelServerPromotionHttpUploaderOptions` exists (a configured real uploader).
- A grep of `27.TranslationDomain/TranslationMachine.cs` for `0.03` returns ZERO hits, the three sites read `DpoTrainer.MinimumPreferenceMargin`, and `11.DPO/DpoTrainer.cs` declares exactly one such public constant.

FAIL after the fix (these must no longer hold):
- Any assertion that pins the null `MeasurementGateRequest` default as the production behaviour.
- Any assertion that pins the `LocalOnlyTrainingIntakePromotionUploader` no-op as the ONLY production uploader.

**5. Effort estimate.** MEDIUM — roughly 6–10 hours of CODE work. Rationale: the config plumbing dominates (threading a configuration-sourced `MeasurementGateRequest` through the production training/promotion path, plus wiring the HTTP-uploader options from host configuration); the margin single-sourcing — one constant, one using-directive, three replacements — is trivial.

**BOUNDARY — TRAINING WORK, DEFERRED.** The actual threshold NUMBERS (what held-out score, runtime, cost, and RAM budget count as "good enough") and the real held-out metrics that make the gate genuinely pass require measurement runs on real training. They are DEFERRED; do NOT invent numbers. STOP at this line for anything requiring measured data.

