### WL-07 — Four training rows born from a forbidden closed model claim "no external AI was used": mark them for rebuild and make the guard distrust the name

**1. The problem.**

The folder `training-data/orchestration-reasoning/` holds preference-training examples — DPO (Direct Preference Optimization) pairs, each a "chosen" good answer set against a "rejected" bad one — for the orchestration-reasoning skill. Four of its source rows are named after, and openly credit, a closed AI model that the project's legal policy forbids as an authoring source. A "closed model" here means Claude / Sonnet, a hosted model whose output the policy bans from becoming training data. The four rows are `or01u-sonnet-clinerules-001`, `or01u-sonnet-cursorrules-001`, `or01u-sonnet-gitattributes-001`, and `or01u-sonnet-gitignore-001`. Three of them — clinerules, gitignore, gitattributes — are carried as the live common-leg payload rows (`orchestration-reasoning-dpo-trainer-payloads.jsonl:1`, `:2`, `:3`); the fourth, cursorrules, is held one step back as an excluded adaptive candidate (`orchestration-reasoning-common-corpus-manifest.json:25`). Each row names its own origin as a source family literally called "sonnet-local-analyzes" — analysis notes a Sonnet model produced — in both its `source_ref` field and its `project_adapter_refs` list (`orchestration-reasoning-dpo-trainer-payloads.jsonl:1`, which reads `"source_ref":"sonnet-local-analyzes:.clinerules-analysis.md"` and `"project_adapter_refs":["source_family:sonnet-local-analyzes", …]`).

Yet every one of those rows also carries the flag `"external_ai_used":false` (`orchestration-reasoning-dpo-trainer-payloads.jsonl:1`), and so does every manifest in the folder (`orchestration-reasoning-dpo-trainer-payloads-manifest.json:22`; `orchestration-reasoning-dpo-pairs-manifest.json:36`). The row's own label therefore contradicts its own name: the source is literally called "sonnet", yet the row asserts no external AI was used.

Nothing in the folder marks these rows for rebuild or quarantine. A grep of `training-data/orchestration-reasoning/` for "rebuild", "contaminat", or "quarantin" returns zero matches. Instead the manifests carry a `status` field whose value is an accepting one — "PASS" (`orchestration-reasoning-common-corpus-manifest.json:5`) or "PASS_PENDING_PARENT_REVIEW" (`orchestration-reasoning-dpo-pairs-manifest.json:5`; `orchestration-reasoning-dpo-trainer-payloads-manifest.json:5`) — the opposite of a quarantine-for-rebuild mark. So the count of rows marked for rebuild is zero while the count found is four.

The code that consumes this corpus — the orchestration-reasoning trainer at `43.SmartSkillsDomain/OrchestrationReasoningTrainer.cs`, whose entry point `MaterializeAsync(payloadPath, manifestPath)` (`:34`) reads a payload file and its manifest — has exactly one guard against forbidden-model authorship, and it is a self-declaration check. At the manifest level it rejects only when the manifest's own `external_ai_used` flag reads true: `if (RequiredBool(manifest, "external_ai_used")) return …Fail(…"ORCHESTRATION_REASONING_TRAINER_EXTERNAL_AI_REJECTED")` (`OrchestrationReasoningTrainer.cs:63–64`). At the row level it likewise rejects a single row only when that same flag reads true, folded into a combined condition (`OrchestrationReasoningTrainer.cs:151`). Because the sonnet rows and manifests all declare `external_ai_used:false`, neither guard fires. The one guard built to stop closed-model authorship trusts the row's self-declaration and never looks at the row's name — so a row whose name and source family both shout "sonnet" sails straight through.

One mitigating fact, stated plainly so it is not mistaken for a fix: none of the four rows is currently promoted to train-ready. A grep shows no `"dpo_train_ready":true` and no `"training_started":true` anywhere in the folder; every row and manifest carries `dpo_train_ready:false` and `training_started:false` (`orchestration-reasoning-dpo-trainer-payloads.jsonl:1`; `orchestration-reasoning-dpo-pairs-manifest.json:31–32`). So nothing contaminated is feeding a live training run at this commit. But that "not yet promoted" state is a general holding state applied to every row in these files, clean non-sonnet rows included — it is not the rebuild marking the policy requires, and it does not make the marked-equals-found count (0 == 4) pass.

**2. The fix.** Two concrete, code-and-data-marking changes (rebuilding the rows' actual content is out of scope — see section 5).

(i) *Data-hygiene marking.* Add an explicit rebuild/quarantine marking to the four sonnet rows and to each manifest that carries them — for example a `"status":"REBUILD_REQUIRED_CONTAMINATED_SOURCE"` field on each of the four source rows, and a matching top-level marking replacing or annotating the accepting `status` values at `orchestration-reasoning-common-corpus-manifest.json:5`, `orchestration-reasoning-dpo-pairs-manifest.json:5`, and `orchestration-reasoning-dpo-trainer-payloads-manifest.json:5`. With four rows marked, the policy's marked-equals-found count gate reads 4 == 4 and passes; today it reads 0 == 4 and fails. This is a hygiene MARK only — it records the defect; it does not author any replacement content.

(ii) *Guard hardening in the consuming trainer.* Add a source-name cross-check to `OrchestrationReasoningTrainer` so that a row or manifest whose identifier or source family names a closed model — case-insensitive "sonnet" or "claude" appearing in `pair_id` / `source_row_id` / `source_ref` / `project_adapter_refs`, or in the manifest's `included_*_row_ids` and `source_refs` — is REJECTED regardless of its self-declared `external_ai_used:false`, with a new, distinct error code such as `ORCHESTRATION_REASONING_TRAINER_CONTAMINATED_SOURCE_REJECTED`. Add the manifest-level check alongside the existing self-declaration guard at `OrchestrationReasoningTrainer.cs:63–64`, and the row-level check alongside the row guard at `:151`. The guard must distrust a self-declaration that contradicts the source's own name: when the name says "sonnet" and the flag says "no external AI", the name wins and the payload is refused.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).**

GOOD — the "chosen" behaviour the fix should make normal: the trainer is handed the payload file containing row `or01u-sonnet-clinerules-001` and refuses it outright with an explicit contaminated-source error that names the offending row — the closed-model name in the source is treated as disqualifying on its own, before any train-ready question is even reached.

BAD — the "rejected" behaviour the fix must make impossible (this is today's behaviour): the same row passes BOTH guard checks — the manifest guard at `OrchestrationReasoningTrainer.cs:63–64` and the row guard at `:151` — purely because it politely claims `external_ai_used:false`, and the ONLY thing standing between a Sonnet-authored row and a training run is the accidental "nothing is train-ready yet" holding state (`dpo_train_ready:false`), not any deliberate quarantine.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL** (no training runs required for any of these).

PASS after the fix:
- A unit test that feeds the trainer a synthetic manifest-plus-row whose source is named "sonnet-…" while carrying `external_ai_used:false` asserts that `MaterializeAsync` fails with the new contaminated-source error code (`ORCHESTRATION_REASONING_TRAINER_CONTAMINATED_SOURCE_REJECTED`).
- A folder-level count assertion over `training-data/orchestration-reasoning/` asserting that the number of rows marked for rebuild equals the number of sonnet-named source rows found: 4 == 4.

FAIL after the fix (these must no longer hold):
- Any test that asserts today's acceptance of a false-flagged sonnet row — that a row named `or01u-sonnet-*` carrying `external_ai_used:false` is materialized successfully.
- The current 0 == 4 state of the marked-equals-found gate (zero rows marked while four are found).

**5. Effort estimate.** SMALL — roughly 2–4 hours of CODE-and-data-marking work. Rationale: one new cross-check helper plus two call sites in a single trainer file, and a `status` marking added to four rows and three manifests — no new subsystem, no schema redesign, no training machinery touched.

**BOUNDARY — TRAINING WORK, DEFERRED.** Actually REBUILDING the four rows' chosen/rejected content from a legally-clean (non-closed-model) source is training-data production, not code: it is DEFERRED and nothing is estimated for it here. This entry stops at MARKING the rows for rebuild and hardening the guard. STOP at this line for anything that would author replacement training content.

