### WL-08 — Every domain writes its passport under one shared file name and the loader lets the last one silently win: add the collision guard, domain scoping, and corrupt-file survival

**1. The problem.** Every trained capability writes a "manifest" — the passport file (a small JSON document) describing one trained checkpoint: which model file, which tokenizer, which domain (subject area), which unit identifier (the string that names one taught capability), and which training settings rebuild it. ALL capabilities write that passport under ONE shared file name, `generated_capability_manifest.json` — the constant `GeneratedCapabilityManifestFileName` at `18.CapabilityTraining\TeachingRegistryPersistence.cs:18` — each dropped into its own per-(run, domain, sub-phase) sub-folder. The on-disk layout is `{root}/{run}/{domain}/{sub-phase}/generated_capability_manifest.json`, built at `18.CapabilityTraining\DefaultLogicalCapabilityTrainer.cs:269` (the checkpoint directory) and `:275` (the passport path).

On first use the trainer fills its in-memory index once — "hydration", the method `EnsureManifestCacheHydrated` — by walking the WHOLE checkpoint folder tree and grabbing every file that carries that one name at any depth (`Directory.GetFiles(..., SearchOption.AllDirectories)` at `:586`, where `SearchOption.AllDirectories` means search by file name through every sub-folder, any depth), then filing each passport into a dictionary keyed by the unit identifier. The filing is a blind overwrite — `_manifestCacheByUnitId[manifest.UnitId] = manifest;` at `:591` — with no duplicate check, no domain filter at load time, and no defined order: the .NET file-listing API follows the filesystem's own enumeration, which the code never sorts, so which duplicate wins is undefined. There is also no per-file error handling — the read-and-parse at `:588-589` (`File.ReadAllText` then `JsonSerializer.Deserialize`) sits in no try/catch, so one corrupt or schema-mismatched JSON throws a parse error that propagates straight out and aborts the WHOLE hydration; and because the "already hydrated" flag is set at `:582` BEFORE the loop, a throw leaves a partially filled index permanently flagged complete — the early-return at the top of the method never re-attempts it.

Collisions are real on two ordinary paths. The unit identifier is assembled as `role + sanitized-domain + sanitized-sub-phase + sanitized-run` (`:270`), so (a) a same-run re-teach of the same domain + sub-phase produces the identical unit id AND the identical folder, clobbering both the file on disk and the index slot; and (b) the `Sanitize` helper (`:921-926`) lowercases a label and turns every non-letter / non-digit character into a hyphen (collapsing runs of hyphens), so genuinely different labels — "Legal EU", "legal-eu", "legal/eu" — all collapse into the one slug "legal-eu", and two distinct domains then silently overwrite each other on disk AND in the index. The replay reader `FindReplayManifests` (`:596-611`) does filter by domain, but only at query time — the `DomainTag == requested-domain` filter at `:602`, reading from the already-polluted shared index — so it cannot recover a passport the overwrite already destroyed. The same by-name-everywhere sweep recurs once more in the experiments runner (`experiments-v2\XIIGEN-DOCS-BATCH-COMPARISON\runner\XiigenDocsBatchComparisonRunner\Program.cs:723`), inside `BuildLoraAudit` — evidence that the pattern repeats, not production code.

**2. The fix.** Four concrete changes:

(i) *Duplicate-id guard.* Replace the blind indexer write `_manifestCacheByUnitId[manifest.UnitId] = manifest;` (`:591`) with an explicit check — `TryAdd` / `ContainsKey` — that, on a second passport carrying an already-seen unit id, either REFUSES (records a named conflict result and does not guess a winner) or resolves the winner loudly and deterministically (for example newest `CreatedAtUtc`, with the loser recorded). Never silent last-one-wins.

(ii) *Domain scoping at LOAD time.* Restrict the recursive sweep to the domain's own sub-tree — the folder layout `{root}/{run}/{domain}/{sub-phase}` already encodes the domain, so the loader can search under the domain's own folder instead of the whole root — OR check the passport's own domain tag (`manifest.DomainTag`) right after parsing and drop any foreign-domain file before it can enter the index. Equivalently, give the passport a domain-scoped file NAME (embed the sanitized domain in the file name) so the by-name sweep can no longer cross domains at its root.

(iii) *Defined corrupt-file behaviour.* Wrap each file's read-and-parse (`:588-589`) in a per-file try/catch: on a bad file, skip it with a recorded note and keep loading the rest; and set the "hydrated" flag only after a clean pass — never leave `_manifestCacheHydrated = true` (`:582`) cementing a partial or failed hydration.

(iv) *Deterministic ordering.* Sort the file list (for example by `CreatedAtUtc` then unit id) before the fill loop so any surviving winner is defined rather than filesystem-order-dependent. This is MOOT if duplicates are refused outright under (i) — stated so explicitly.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).** GOOD "chosen" (the behaviour to train toward): two passports carrying the SAME unit identifier under two different sub-folders → hydration reports a NAMED conflict (which unit id, which two files) and REFUSES to guess a winner, instead of silently keeping whichever the filesystem happened to list last. A corrupt passport dropped anywhere under the root → the OTHER passports still load, the bad file is skipped with a recorded skip note, and the index is NOT flagged hydrated on a failed pass. BAD "rejected" (today's behaviour, which must become impossible): domain "Legal EU"'s passport silently REPLACES domain "legal/eu"'s passport because both sanitize to "legal-eu" (`:921-926`) — same slug, same unit id (`:270`), same file — and the losing domain's replay history simply vanishes from the index, unrecoverable even though the replay reader filters by domain (`:602`); and one corrupt or schema-mismatched file kills the entire index build (the throw at `:588-589`) while the code has already sworn hydration succeeded (the flag set at `:582`), so a retry never repairs it.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL** (no training runs required for any of these). PASS after the fix: a test that drops two passports with the SAME unit id under two sub-folders asserts a conflict result (a rejection, or a deterministically-named winner with the loser recorded) rather than a single silent survivor; a test that places a foreign-domain passport under the checkpoint root asserts it NEVER enters the target domain's cache (nor its replay set); a test that drops malformed JSON under the root asserts the good passports still load AND a skip note is recorded for the bad file, rather than an aborted hydration. FAIL after the fix (these must no longer hold): any assertion that pins today's silent last-one-wins overwrite — a duplicate unit id yielding one silent survivor; any assertion that pins the abort-and-cement-partial-cache behaviour — one corrupt file throwing out of hydration while the index stays flagged complete.

**5. Effort estimate.** SMALL — roughly 3–5 hours of pure code work. Rationale: four localized changes in one trainer file (a dictionary-write policy, a domain filter or a domain-scoped search, a per-file try/catch, and an optional deterministic sort), plus a handful of unit tests over synthetic on-disk passports — no schema redesign, no new subsystem, no training machinery touched. **This item has NO deferred-training boundary — the entire fix is code** (file listing, JSON parsing, dictionary-key policy, and folder / file naming and scoping); nothing needs to STOP for data or a training run.

