### WL-09 — The code's two honest self-confessions: an episode memory that forgets on restart, and a hardware monitor that exists only as a test stub

**1. The problem.** Two spots in the code openly admit, in their own comments, that they are unfinished — and both sit at the far edge of the system, away from any preference-training maths.

*(a) The episode memory that forgets on restart.* The orchestrator (the component that breaks a big task into sub-tasks and coordinates the work) keeps a diary of its past runs so it can look back at similar tasks. That diary is `InMemoryEpisodeMemory` — "episode memory held only in the running program's working memory" (`15.OrchestratorModel/Core/InMemoryEpisodeMemory.cs:13`). It genuinely works: `Store` (save a task and how it was broken down, `:28`) and `Recall` (find past tasks whose text contains a search word, `:43`) both do their job, keeping entries in a plain in-memory list (`:16-17`). The catch is that the list lives only inside the running process, so every entry vanishes the moment the program restarts. The code says so itself: "Stub implementation — real persistence comes with 14.ContinuousLearning" (`InMemoryEpisodeMemory.cs:11`) and, on the contract it fulfils, "InMemoryEpisodeMemory is a stub until 14.ContinuousLearning" (`15.OrchestratorModel/Core/Interfaces.cs:53`). A "stub" is a stand-in that fills the slot but does not do the durable job; a store that survives a restart still has to be written.

*(b) The hardware monitor that exists only as a test stand-in.* The autonomous-learning scheduler is allowed to start a training cycle only when the machine is quiet enough — low CPU load, the user away from the keyboard, on mains power, enough free disk. To know that, it must read the machine's live hardware state. That reading is promised by the contract `ISystemMonitor` — "system monitor" (`16.AutonomousLearning/Core/Interfaces.cs:33`), whose single method `Sample` (`:36`) returns a `SystemStatus` snapshot of CPU percentage, minutes since the last key or mouse event, whether it is charging, battery percentage and free disk (`:12-17`). Its own comment confesses: "Real implementation reads OS metrics; test stub returns configured values" (`:32`). A tree-wide search — excluding build output (`bin`, `obj`) and the worktree copies, and treating the `Tests\` folder as test-only — confirms the ONLY thing that implements this contract is a scripted test double, `StubSystemMonitorAL` (`Tests/AutonomousLearningTests.cs:552`), which merely hands back a fixed snapshot it was given in its constructor (`:558-562`) without ever asking the operating system. The pipeline that consumes the monitor (`16.AutonomousLearning/Pipeline/AutonomousLearningPipeline.cs:30, 51`) is only a user of the contract, not a real implementer. The real operating-system-metrics reader does not exist.

Both are pure code and peripheral: neither calculates any training loss. The first only feeds the orchestrator's recall of past runs; the second only decides WHEN a learning cycle may begin.

**2. The fix.** Two independent pieces of code, each implementing an already-defined contract so nothing around them has to change:

(i) *A persistence-backed episode store.* Write a new class that fulfils the same `IEpisodeMemory` contract (`Store` + `Recall`) but keeps its entries on disk or in a database — reusing the persistence layer the codebase already has (`ISessionStorage`, the same storage abstraction this class already takes as its first constructor argument at `InMemoryEpisodeMemory.cs:23`) — so that an episode saved before a restart is still there afterwards. When it lands, the two "stub until 14.ContinuousLearning" comments (`InMemoryEpisodeMemory.cs:11`, `Interfaces.cs:53`) are deleted.

(ii) *A production system monitor.* Write a real class implementing `ISystemMonitor.Sample` that actually reads the five live numbers from the operating system — CPU load (sampled over a short interval), idle time since the last input event, charging state, battery level and free disk on the training volume — replacing the "test stub returns configured values" clause (`Interfaces.cs:32`) with a class that queries the machine.

**3. Positive and negative example (preference-training spirit).** GOOD "chosen" (the behaviour to steer toward): save an episode through one store instance, discard that instance, construct a FRESH store instance over the same on-disk storage, and `Recall` returns the saved episode — memory survives the object's lifetime; and the monitor's two back-to-back samples show live, changing hardware numbers (CPU load and idle minutes differ from one reading to the next). BAD "rejected" (today's behaviour, which must become impossible): a process restart silently wipes the entire episode diary while the orchestrator still believes it remembers its past runs; and an "idle detector" that, if wired into production, would only ever see the same pre-scripted constants, because the sole implementer is a test double that echoes a fixed snapshot.

**4. Tests that should PASS / FAIL.** No training run is involved in any of these. PASS after the fix: a survival test — save an episode, build a fresh store over the same storage, assert `Recall` returns it; a repo-wide search that now finds a production (non-test) class implementing `ISystemMonitor`, not only `StubSystemMonitorAL` in `Tests\`; and a sampling test asserting the monitor returns live values that differ between two calls, rather than the scripted constants a stub echoes. FAIL after the fix: honestly, nothing in the current test suite pins the wrong behaviour, so there is no red assertion to point at — the "red" side here is the disappearance of the two written confessions. A search for the "stub until 14.ContinuousLearning" comments (`InMemoryEpisodeMemory.cs:11`, `Interfaces.cs:53`) and the "test stub returns configured values" comment (`16.AutonomousLearning/Core/Interfaces.cs:32`) must return zero hits once the real implementations exist.

**5. Effort estimate.**
- *(i) Episode-memory persistence* — **SMALL, ~4–6 hours.** The contract has only two methods and the persistence abstraction it needs (`ISessionStorage`) is already injected; the only real work is serialising the task-and-decomposition entries, carrying the eviction rule across restarts, and adding the fresh-instance survival test.
- *(ii) Real system monitor* — **MEDIUM, ~6–10 hours.** Five distinct hardware readings, several needing operating-system-specific plumbing (input-idle time and battery/charging state via platform calls, CPU load sampled over an interval, free disk from the drive), plus cross-platform care and a live-values test.

**These two items have NO deferred-training boundary — both are pure code.** They must not be confused with Finding 43's bucket-(b) items — the training-data provider (`ITrainingDataProvider`), the accuracy evaluator (`IAccuracyEvaluator`), and the placeholder training provenance in the trainable router — which sit behind the deferred-training line and are deliberately NOT estimated in this entry.

